PowerShell and scheduled-task telemetry in full, identity and account-management events from Security, service installs and crashes from System and Application, and Defender detections. What it leaves out is just as deliberate: no 4688 process creation, no object access (4656–4663), no 4697, no audit-policy change, nothing from WMI-Activity, RDP session, NTLM, AppLocker or CodeIntegrity channels. The agent assumes its own EDR telemetry covers process and file activity


If you are running another collector to pull windows event logs, it is important to see the total, overall coverage of the logs:

// Every event arriving from every Windows log, per collector, provider, event ID and outcome/level.
// 7-day window. Swap the dataset for microsoft_sysmon_raw to get the same view of Sysmon.
//       all other providers = log level (information / warning / error / verbose / critical).
dataset = microsoft_windows_raw
| alter h    = lowercase(arrayindex(regextract(host_name, "^([^.]+)"), 0)),
        src  = if(_collector_type = "Cortex Agent", "agent", "cribl"),
        log  = coalesce(channel, "(operational)"),
        kind = if(provider_name = "Microsoft-Windows-Security-Auditing",
                  if(_collector_type = "Cortex Agent", "n/a (agent)",
                     if(_raw_log contains "Audit Failure", "Failure", "Success")),
                  coalesce(log_level, LevelDisplayName, to_string(Level), "-"))
| comp count() as events, count_distinct(h) as hosts, min(_time) as first_seen, max(_time) as last_seen
       by src, log, provider_name, event_id, kind
| alter lk = 1
| join type = left (
    dataset = microsoft_windows_raw
    | alter fk = 1, h = lowercase(arrayindex(regextract(host_name, "^([^.]+)"), 0))
    | comp count_distinct(h) as fleet by fk
  ) as f f.fk = lk
| alter pct_hosts = round(multiply(divide(hosts, fleet), 100))
| alter coverage = if(pct_hosts >= 80, "fleet-wide", if(pct_hosts >= 5, "partial", "few hosts"))
| alter per_host_day = round(divide(divide(events, hosts), 7))
| fields src, log, provider_name, event_id, kind, coverage, pct_hosts, hosts, events, per_host_day, first_seen, last_seen
| sort asc src, asc log, asc provider_name, asc event_id, asc kind
| limit 5000

UNDERSTANDING THE RESULTS:
The output is the complete inventory of what the SIEM receives from Windows event logs. Gaps are found by reading it three ways.

By src — what each collector contributes. Filter src = "{insert}" and look at the distinct log values. Filter src = "agent" and log = "(operational)": that is the full list of Operational channels you have, and it is short — PowerShell, TaskScheduler, LDAP-Client, PrintService, Firewall, Defender (1116/1117 only), CAPI2, Directory Service. Any channel you expect and do not see in either list is collected by nobody. 

By coverage and pct_hosts — whether a log reaches the fleet or a corner of it. fleet-wide is what it says. partial on a Security ID is usually the event being rare, not the policy being off. Partial on an Operational channel is a profile scope problem. Few hosts on a Security ID that should be everywhere is an audit-policy inconsistency.

By kind — success, failure, or both. Two rows for the same event_id (one Success, one Failure) means the subcategory is set to both; one row means one side only. 4625 only ever appears as Failure, 4624 only as Success — that is the event, not the policy. A subcategory with no rows at all is off or had no activity in the window; widen to 30 days before calling it off.

By per_host_day — what each row costs. Sort descending. 

Overlap. Same provider_name + event_id appearing under both src values is a double copy. 

The short version: the rows tell you what arrives; the absent rows tell you what doesn't; src tells you who to fix when something is missing.
